g_0e64608749aa6838016acead8a86a887d28d7c9a7644298051","type":"message","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"output_text","annotations":[],"logprobs":[],"text":"It suffices to find **three integer terms** to establish option (D).\n\nBy Legendre’s formula,\n\\[\nv_p(m!)=\\sum_{k\\ge1}\\left\\lfloor\\frac{m}{p^k}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nThe \\(n\\)-th term is an integer exactly when\n\\[\nv_p((n^2+1)!)\\ge(n+2)v_p(n!)\n\\]\nfor every prime \\(p\\le n\\); larger primes do not divide the denominator.\n\nFor \\(n=1\\), the term equals \\(2\\). Two further integer terms are verified below; entries list valuations in the indicated prime order:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\nn&\\text{primes}&v_p((n^2+1)!)&(n+2)v_p(n!)\\\\ \\hline\n6&(2,3,5)&(34,17,8)&(32,16,8)\\\\\n10&(2,3,5,7)&(97,48,24,16)&(96,48,24,12)\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nEvery numerator valuation is at least the corresponding denominator valuation. Thus the terms for \\(n=1,6,10\\) are all integers, so there are more than two integer terms.\n\nFinal answer: **(D) More than 2.**"}],"phase":"final_answer","role":"assistant"}],"parallel_tool_calls":true,"presence_penalty":0,"previous_response_id":null,"prompt_cache_key":null,"prompt_cache_retention":"24h","reasoning":{"context":"all_turns","effort":"medium","mode":"standard","summary":null},"safety_identifier":null,"service_tier":"default","store":false,"temperature":1,"text":{"format":{"type":"text"},"verbosity":"medium"},"tool_choice":"auto","tool_usage":{"image_gen":{"input_tokens":0,"input_tokens_details":{"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":0},"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":{"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":0},"total_tokens":0},"web_search":{"num_requests":0}},"tools":[],"top_logprobs":0,"top_p":0.98,"truncation":"disabled","usage":{"input_tokens":157,"input_tokens_details":{"cache_write_tokens":0,"cached_tokens":0},"output_tokens":769,"output_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":466},"total_tokens":926},"user":null,"metadata":{}}
